<p class="lead">
  Smallpdf is a well-designed, capable tool — and its free tier is deliberately narrow: two tasks per day before it asks you to subscribe. For occasional use that's a minor inconvenience; for anyone processing more than a couple of files in one sitting, it becomes the actual bottleneck. Here's what to reach for instead, depending on what you actually need.
</p>

<h2>If you want no account and no daily cap</h2>
<p>
  <a href="/">keptlocal</a> runs every operation in your browser instead of uploading to a server, which is also why there's no daily task limit to hit — there's no per-task server cost to manage on the free tier, because there's no server-side processing happening at all. Covers merge, split, compress, watermark, protect, convert to image, fill forms, and more, across 46 tools total, with no signup at any point.
</p>

<h2>If you specifically need OCR or PDF-to-Word</h2>
<p>
  This is the clearest case where Smallpdf's server-side processing is genuinely doing something a browser-only tool can't yet replicate well. If your recurring need is converting scanned documents to editable Word files or running OCR on scans, Smallpdf (or a comparable service like iLovePDF) remains the more capable choice for that specific job — worth keeping as a bookmark for those particular tasks even if you handle everything else with a browser-based tool.
</p>

<h2>If you want a desktop application instead of a website</h2>
<p>
  <strong>PDF24 Creator</strong> is a free desktop application for Windows that bundles a large set of PDF tools locally on your machine — an option if you'd rather install software once than rely on any website, browser-based or not, and don't mind a less polished interface in exchange for a broader feature set installed locally.
</p>

<h2>If you need advanced editing (redaction, OCR, forms design)</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Adobe Acrobat</strong> remains the industry standard for genuinely advanced PDF work — real redaction (not just visual black boxes, but removing the underlying data), building interactive forms from scratch, and Bates numbering for legal document sets. It's a paid product, but for professional workflows that need these specific capabilities, it's the tool built for them.
</p>

